The **fork** system call is a feature of Unix-like operating systems that creates a new process by duplicating the calling process. `fork()` creates a new process that is an exact copy of its parent in every way except the process ID.

Both processes continue execution from the point of the `fork()` call. The parent receives the PID of the child, while the child receives 0, indicating that it is the child. After `fork()`, one of them (usually the child) uses `exec()`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid;
    pid = fork(); /* fork a child process */
    if (pid < 0) {
        /* error */
    } else if (pid == 0) {
        /* child */
        execlp("/bin/ls", "ls", NULL);
    } else {
        /* parent will wait for child */
        wait(NULL);
        printf("child complete");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The **critical section problem** is a synchronization problem that arises when two or more processes need to access a shared resource simultaneously, while only one process may use it at a given time.

---

*Another solution in the source (for 2012 M Q(b)):*

**Peterson's solution** is a classic software-based solution to the critical section problem. It is restricted to two processes that alternate execution between their critical sections and remainder sections.

---

*Another solution in the source (for 2018 E Q(b)):*

- The two processes $P_i$ and $P_j$ share a `flag` array, with each flag set to false on initialization. `flag[i]` indicates whether process $P_i$ is ready to enter the critical section.
- The `turn` variable denotes whose turn it is to enter the critical section.
- Every process enters the entry section, in which $j$ denotes the other process.
- Process $i$ sets `flag[i] = true` and gives the turn to the other process (`turn = j`).
- Process $i$ then waits while `flag[j] == true && turn == j`. If $j$ is in its critical section, the while loop runs continuously and stalls $i$ until $j$ exits.
- A process leaving its critical section sets its own flag to false (`flag[i] = false`).

```c
int turn;
boolean flag[2];
```

Structure of process $P_i$:

```c
do {
    flag[i] = true;
    turn = j;
    while (flag[j] && turn == j);
    /* CRITICAL SECTION */
    flag[i] = false;
    /* REMAINDER SECTION */
} while (TRUE);
```

Structure of process $P_j$:

```c
do {
    flag[j] = true;
    turn = i;
    while (flag[i] && turn == i);
    /* CRITICAL SECTION */
    flag[j] = false;
    /* REMAINDER SECTION */
} while (TRUE);
```

